with d as (select value r from jsonb_array_elements($q$[{"notes":["Make the jalebi syrup and batter the day before if you like, this takes the stress out of frying day. Keep both in airtight containers at room temperature.","The garlic chutney keeps in the fridge for up to 3 days.","Fafda is best eaten warm and fresh, but jalebi will keep in the syrup for up to 2 days."],"steps":[{"text":"Make the jalebi syrup first: In a heavy-bottomed saucepan, combine the caster sugar and 300 ml water. Bring to a boil, stirring until the sugar dissolves completely. Add the lemon juice and crushed cardamom pods. If using saffron, crumble it into the syrup. Simmer for 10 minutes until the syrup thickens slightly and coats the back of a spoon. Set aside to cool completely."},{"text":"Make the jalebi batter: In a bowl, whisk together the plain flour and bicarbonate of soda. Add the yoghurt and water, stirring until you have a smooth, lump-free batter with a consistency like double cream, thin enough to flow through a piping bag, but not watery. If using saffron, crumble it into the batter. Stir in the 2 tbsp of neutral oil. Set aside for 15 minutes to rest."},{"text":"Make the garlic chutney: Blend all the ingredients together in a food processor or with a mortar and pestle until you reach a smooth paste. Transfer to a small bowl and set aside."},{"text":"Make the fafda batter: In a large bowl, whisk together the gram flour, yoghurt, salt, turmeric and cumin. Add the chopped green chilli. Gradually add the 200 ml water, stirring until you have a thick, smooth batter, it should be slightly thicker than the jalebi batter, dropping slowly from a spoon. Gently fold in the carrot, peas and red onion. Let the batter rest for 10 minutes."},{"text":"Heat the 1 litre of neutral oil in a heavy-bottomed, deep saucepan or wok to 170°C. You can check the temperature with a thermometer; if you don't have one, a small piece of batter should sizzle gently and brown in about 60 seconds."},{"text":"Fry the jalebi: Transfer the jalebi batter to a piping bag fitted with a thin nozzle (about 5 mm). Carefully squeeze the batter into the hot oil in spiral shapes, starting from the centre and spiralling outward, making each jalebi about 5 cm across. Fry 3–4 at a time so they don't touch. Fry for about 90 seconds until deep golden and crisp, then turn gently and fry the other side for another 30–45 seconds. Remove with a slotted spoon and immediately place in the cool jalebi syrup. Repeat until all the batter is used. Set the jalebi aside in the syrup."},{"text":"Fry the fafda: Once the oil returns to 170°C, carefully lower a fafda mould (or use a kitchen spoon or ladle) into the oil if you have one. Alternatively, use your hands or two spoons to shape the batter into loose, irregular nests about 10 cm across, don't make them perfectly compact; fafda should be airy and crispy. Fry 2–3 at a time for 3–4 minutes, turning halfway, until golden and crispy all over. The edges should be lacy and crackly. Remove with a slotted spoon and drain on paper towel."},{"text":"Serve the fafda warm with a jalebi on the side, a spoonful of garlic chutney, and a small bowl of the syrup for dipping."}],"id":"b2e46c60-cf60-41db-99ff-5f4de83ba0d2"},{"notes":["Make the wors up to 2 days ahead and store in the fridge. They freeze beautifully too.","If you don't have natural casings, make pattie wors instead: shape the mixture into thick patties and braai or pan-fry them.","Pap reheats well, if it thickens too much overnight, loosen it with a splash of water or milk on the stove.","Food safety: Cook mince, sausages, meatballs and liver right through to 75°C, with no pink in the centre."],"id":"434c23a8-59af-4d37-a327-3fde109af852"},{"notes":["Make it a day ahead: flavour deepens overnight. Reheat gently on the stove with a splash of water if needed.","For deeper colour and flavour, fry the paste longer in step 2, dark brown is better than light.","Serve with steamed white rice or fragrant jasmine rice to soak up the sauce.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C."],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the shallots, garlic, ginger, galangal and dried chillies with 100 ml water until smooth. This is your rendang paste."},{"text":"Heat the oil in a large, heavy-based pot over medium heat. Add the paste and caraway seeds. Fry, stirring often, until fragrant and the oil begins to separate from the paste, about 8 minutes."},{"text":"Add the chicken pieces and stir to coat well in the paste. Cook for 5 minutes, stirring, until the chicken is sealed."},{"text":"Pour in the coconut milk and stir. Add the turmeric, lime leaves and a pinch of salt. Bring to a gentle simmer until the sauce is bubbling lightly and fragrant."},{"text":"Reduce heat to low. Simmer uncovered for 60 minutes, stirring every 10 minutes or so. The sauce will gradually reduce and thicken, and the oil will begin to separate again, this is what you want."},{"text":"Keep simmering until the sauce is thick, glossy and coats the chicken, and the chicken is very tender. Taste and adjust the salt. Serve with rice."}],"id":"1ec8f967-bbeb-4b34-939a-9d910417d33b"},{"notes":["Use the freshest white fish and seafood you can find, suquet is a stew that lets them shine.","The sofregit is the soul of this dish. Those 20 minutes of slow cooking transform the onions into something deep and essential.","Serve with aioli or toum on the side if you like, a dollop in each bowl is very Catalan.","Food safety: Cook fish until it turns opaque and flakes easily, about 63°C in the thickest part. Cook mussels, clams and pipis only if they are alive and tightly closed (or close when tapped), and discard any that stay shut after cooking. Cook prawns until pink and opaque."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the olive oil in a large, heavy-bottomed pot or cazuela over a low-medium heat. Add the sliced onions and cook, stirring often, for 15 to 20 minutes until very soft and golden. This is the sofregit, don't rush it. The onions should be almost jammy. Add the garlic and cook for another 2 minutes until fragrant."},{"text":"Stir in the crushed tomatoes and paprika. Cook gently for 5 minutes, letting the tomato meld with the sofregit base."},{"text":"Add the potatoes and fish stock. Bring to a simmer and cook for 12 to 15 minutes until the potatoes are almost tender."},{"text":"Add the white fish chunks and mussels. Simmer gently for 4 minutes, then add the prawns. Cook for another 3 to 4 minutes until the fish is just opaque and cooked through, the mussels have opened, and the prawns are pink."},{"text":"Discard any mussels that haven't opened. Taste and season with salt and black pepper."},{"text":"Ladle into bowls and scatter with fresh parsley. Serve with crusty bread to soak up the broth."}],"id":"d595ff1e-4fde-4539-8b54-507bcaf9e69a"},{"notes":["Make niter kibbeh at home: melt 200g butter over low heat, add 1 tsp fenugreek seeds, ½ tsp cardamom pods, 2 cloves, 1 bay leaf and a pinch of ground turmeric. Let it infuse for 10 minutes, then strain through muslin into a jar and refrigerate.","Doro wat keeps well: store in the fridge for up to 3 days and reheat gently with a splash of water if needed.","For a thinner sauce, add more water and simmer a few minutes longer. Selam likes her stew thick enough to sit on the injera without running.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"id":"03ee7fb1-ed47-4dce-b19a-fd2f4d693809"},{"notes":["Make these a day ahead and refrigerate, they taste better when the flavours have settled. Bring to room temperature before serving.","If you can't find Aleppo pepper, use a mix of smoked paprika and a pinch of chilli flakes."],"id":"15333b27-52e1-4750-a6bd-ac4758ccd7e0"},{"notes":["Milkfish can be bony: eat slowly and you'll find most of the small bones soften in the vinegar. If you can't find it, use any firm white fish about the same size, barramundi or snapper work well.","Make ahead: braise to completion, cool in the liquid, and refrigerate for up to 3 days. Reheat gently over low heat, covered."],"id":"051076a2-31a0-4c94-ba01-b7e95c8140fb"},{"notes":["Very fresh sardines are essential here. If you can't find them, use fresh anchovies (alici) instead.","The pasta water is crucial, it helps emulsify the oil and coat the spaghetti properly."],"id":"e57baa31-6036-4dfa-8602-2b22cf208820"},{"notes":["Food safety: Cook fish until it turns opaque and flakes easily, about 63°C in the thickest part."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oven to 200°C, then arrange the fish fillets, skin side down, in a baking dish large enough to hold them in a single layer. Season with salt and pepper."},{"text":"Scatter the sliced brown onion over the fish. Pour the coconut cream and water around the fish, then squeeze the juice from the lime halves over the top. Season again lightly with salt and pepper. Cook until the fish is opaque and flakes easily and the onion is soft."},{"text":"Bake, uncovered, for 25–30 minutes, until the fish is cooked through and flakes easily when tested with a fork."},{"text":"Scatter the chopped spring onion over the top and serve hot with the coconut broth spooned over. Serve with steamed rice and pickled onions on the side."}],"id":"25e4e0ed-4767-4b4c-a914-cfec4f89a438"},{"notes":["Swap the carrot for peas, corn, or any cooked veg you have left over.","If you've got leftover cooked chicken, use 400 g and add it after the garlic and ginger, just heat through before the soy mixture.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C. Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C."],"id":"b19933fd-b090-41d2-a252-838c11cc03f2"},{"notes":["This recipe uses cooked chicken for safe home cooking. Traditional lawar is made with raw poultry only when the cook has direct knowledge of the animal's health and handling.","Make base genep fresh if you can: blend together 4 shallots, 3 garlic cloves, 1 tbsp ginger, 1 tsp galangal, 1 tsp ground turmeric, 2–3 red chillies and 1 tbsp coconut oil.","Lawar is best served the same day it's made, on banana leaf if you have it.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear."],"id":"f79bc613-8077-4b60-8553-dacc295ca773"},{"notes":["This gulai keeps well in the fridge for up to 3 days and tastes even better the next day as the spices settle.","Freeze it in portions, it thaws and reheats beautifully.","Use 2 dried chillies for mild heat, 4 for proper burn; it depends on the family eating it that night."],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the dried chillies in hot water for 5 minutes until soft, then drain."},{"text":"Grind the soaked chillies, shallot, garlic, ginger, turmeric and cumin seeds to a paste using a mortar and pestle or spice grinder, adding a little water if needed."},{"text":"Heat the oil in a large pot or wok over medium heat. Fry the paste for 2 to 3 minutes, stirring constantly, until fragrant and darkened slightly."},{"text":"Add the cassava chunks and stir to coat with the paste, about 2 minutes."},{"text":"Pour in the coconut milk and water. Bring to a simmer, then reduce heat to medium-low until the sauce is just bubbling at the edges."},{"text":"Cook, uncovered, for 20 to 25 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the cassava is completely tender and the sauce has thickened slightly. The cassava should break apart easily when tested with a fork."},{"text":"Add the fish sauce and taste. Adjust salt and heat to your preference, remembering the chillies will have released their full flavour by now."},{"text":"Simmer for another minute, until the sauce is thick and glossy and the cassava is tender, then serve hot with steamed rice."}],"id":"915cfa45-ea0b-4e0e-9edd-97d31728cd02"},{"notes":["Make this a day ahead, it reheats beautifully and tastes even better the next day.","Serve with egg noodles, boiled potatoes, or just good bread for soaking up the gravy.","Food safety: Slow-cooked meat is safe once it is tender and the centre has reached at least 75°C."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oven to 160°C. Pat the brisket dry with paper towels and season generously on both sides with salt and pepper."},{"text":"Heat the oil in a large heavy-based pot or Dutch oven over medium-high heat. Working in batches if needed, brown the brisket on all sides until deep golden, about 3 minutes per side. Set the meat aside."},{"text":"Add the sliced onions to the pot and cook, stirring, until they start to soften and caramelize, about 5 minutes. They don't need to be fully cooked, they'll finish in the oven."},{"text":"Pour in the beef stock, scraping up any browned bits from the bottom of the pot. Return the brisket to the pot, nestling it into the onions. Add the bay leaves."},{"text":"Cover the pot with a lid and transfer to the oven. Braise for about 3 hours, until the brisket is fork-tender and the meat shreds easily."},{"text":"Remove from the oven and let the brisket rest in the braising liquid for 10 minutes. Slice against the grain and serve with the soft onions and braising liquid spooned over the top."}],"id":"cb6423c3-4b0e-4589-9c60-bb8ccf0a4f7a"},{"notes":["Serve with rice, cornbread, or just a spoon and good company.","Tastes even better the next day, the flavours deepen as it sits."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat your cast iron over medium-high heat. Brown the andouille slices, 3–4 minutes per side, until they've got a nice colour. Transfer to a plate."},{"text":"Add the oil to the same pan. Sauté the onion until it's soft and golden, about 5 minutes. Stir in the garlic and cook for 1 minute until fragrant."},{"text":"Add the okra and cook, stirring occasionally, for about 8 minutes. Don't fuss with it too much, you want it to brown slightly."},{"text":"Pour in the crushed tomatoes and their juice, then add the corn and chicken stock. Bring to a simmer until the stew is bubbling gently and starting to thicken."},{"text":"Return the sausage to the pan. Reduce heat to medium-low and simmer, uncovered, for 15–20 minutes, stirring gently now and then, until the okra is tender and the flavours have married."},{"text":"Taste and season with salt and pepper. Serve warm."}],"id":"f67d9140-3276-4ef4-af80-d4750017c173"},{"notes":["Rohan likes his pelau hot; remove the green chillies before serving if your household prefers it milder.","Leftover pelau keeps in the fridge for 3 days and reheats beautifully on the stove with a splash of water.","You can swap the chicken for beef chuck or lamb shoulder for a different flavour, both work beautifully and need the full cooking time.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil in a large, heavy-bottomed pot over medium heat. Sprinkle the caster sugar evenly across the base and let it sit without stirring for 2 to 3 minutes. Once it starts to brown at the edges, stir constantly until it's a deep golden brown, almost black, this is the burnt sugar that gives pelau its dark gravy, about 5 to 6 minutes total. It'll smell slightly burnt; that's right."},{"text":"Working quickly, add the chicken pieces to the burnt sugar, stirring constantly to coat them. They'll sizzle and stick a little, that's fine. Cook, stirring, for 3 to 4 minutes until the chicken is sealed on the outside."},{"text":"Add the sliced onions and stir well, scraping up any stuck bits from the bottom of the pot. Cook for 5 to 6 minutes until the onions soften and start to caramelise."},{"text":"Stir in the garlic, ginger and green chillies, and cook for 1 minute until fragrant. Add the ground cumin and ground coriander, stir, and cook for another minute."},{"text":"Add the tomato paste and stir through, coating everything for about 1 minute. Pour in the coconut milk and chicken stock, stirring to loosen any stuck bits. Add the thyme sprigs."},{"text":"Bring to a boil, then reduce the heat to low. Cover and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes until the chicken is cooked through (75°C inside) and the liquid is starting to reduce."},{"text":"Stir in the rice and pigeon peas, mixing well so the rice is submerged. Season with salt and black pepper to taste. Cover and simmer over low heat for 20 to 25 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the rice is tender and has absorbed most of the liquid. If it looks dry but the rice isn't quite cooked, add a little more stock or water."},{"text":"Remove from heat, let it rest, covered, for 5 minutes. Fluff with a fork, sprinkle with fresh coriander, and serve with lime wedges on the side."}],"id":"6511e822-94f4-4660-91bc-a211cfeee277"},{"steps":[{"text":"Heat the mustard oil in a large pan over medium-high heat until it smokes lightly and settles, this takes about 1 minute. This is important; the oil must announce itself."},{"text":"Add the nigella seeds and let them crackle for a few seconds until fragrant."},{"text":"Add the potatoes and stir to coat in oil. Cook for 3–4 minutes until they begin to colour at the edges."},{"text":"Sprinkle in the turmeric and stir well."},{"text":"Add the green chilli and cook, stirring occasionally, for 8–10 minutes until the potatoes are three-quarters cooked and lightly golden."},{"text":"Mix the poppy seed paste with 2 tbsp water to loosen it slightly. Add to the pan and stir to coat the potatoes evenly. If it seems dry, add another tablespoon of water."},{"text":"Cook, stirring gently now and then, for 5–7 minutes until the potatoes are tender and the paste is no longer raw. The dish should be almost dry, with the potatoes well coated in the dark paste."},{"text":"Taste and adjust salt. Serve warm, scattered with fresh coriander if you wish."}],"id":"4c3d1fc0-d6a7-41bf-b36d-a567a11887bf"},{"notes":["This stew reheats beautifully and tastes even better the next day. Keep in the fridge for up to 3 days.","Serve with crusty bread for soaking up the broth, or with plain white rice.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil in a large pot over medium-high heat. Working in batches, brown the lamb on all sides, about 8 minutes total. Set aside."},{"text":"Add the diced onion to the pot and cook, stirring, until soft and golden, about 5 minutes."},{"text":"Return the lamb to the pot and stir in the cumin. Cook for 1 minute until fragrant."},{"text":"Add the tinned tomatoes, breaking them up with the back of a spoon, then pour in the stock and add the lentils. Stir well and bring to a simmer until the liquid is bubbling gently."},{"text":"Reduce the heat to low, cover partially, and simmer for 50 minutes, stirring occasionally. The lentils should be very soft and starting to break down, and the lamb should be tender."},{"text":"Season with salt and pepper to taste. If the stew is too thin, simmer uncovered for a few more minutes until it is thick and the lentils are soft. Serve hot."}],"id":"56aa0ea9-4c71-4ea9-ae3c-e7864e1d9876"},{"notes":["Make ahead: Gumbo tastes even better the next day. Cool completely, refrigerate for up to 3 days, and reheat gently on the stove.","The roux is the foundation, keep the heat medium and stir constantly. A dark roux (not burnt) gives the gumbo its signature flavour and colour.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C."],"steps":[{"text":"Melt butter in a large heavy-bottomed pot over medium heat. Add flour and stir constantly, scraping the bottom, until the roux turns deep chocolate brown, about 25–30 minutes. Don't rush it, burnt roux tastes bitter, so keep the heat medium and stay patient."},{"text":"Add the diced onion, celery and capsicum to the roux. Stir and cook until the vegetables begin to soften, about 5 minutes, scraping up any browned bits from the bottom."},{"text":"Stir in the garlic and cook for 1 minute until fragrant."},{"text":"Add the andouille sausage and cook, stirring, for 3–4 minutes until it releases its oil and the edges colour."},{"text":"Add the chicken thighs and turn to coat in the roux. Cook for 2–3 minutes, then pour in the chicken stock. Add the bay leaves and thyme."},{"text":"Bring to a gentle boil, then reduce heat to low. Simmer, partially covered, for 1 hour, stirring occasionally. The chicken should be very tender and the broth rich and dark."},{"text":"Remove from heat. Pull the chicken thighs from the pot, let cool slightly, shred the meat from the bones and return it to the pot. Discard the bay leaves."},{"text":"Taste and season with salt and black pepper."},{"text":"Serve over cooked white rice in wide bowls with a spoon and fork for the broth and meat."}],"id":"ffe5c95f-a653-43e3-a00c-807c38ffb382"},{"notes":["Make ahead: cook the chicken and prepare the ají paste the day before. Reheat gently before serving.","Use leftover roast or poached chicken to save time on a weeknight.","Fresh ají amarillo peppers are best, but bottled paste (found in Latin American shops) works perfectly well.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"id":"23f994d1-1704-4cfa-a48e-47db8f65ebde"},{"notes":["Make ahead: assemble the pastilla up to 2 hours before baking and refrigerate, covered with cling film. Brush with melted butter just before baking.","The eggs must stay soft and creamy, don't cook them through. They'll cook gently in the oven.","Filo dries out quickly. Keep sheets under a damp tea towel until you use them.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"steps":[{"text":"Place the chicken breast, quartered onion, ginger, turmeric, cinnamon, salt and pepper in a large pot. Cover with water and bring to a simmer. Cook for 20 minutes until the chicken is cooked through. Remove the chicken, let it cool slightly, then shred the meat finely, discarding the skin. Chop the coriander and parsley and set aside."},{"text":"Toast the almonds in a dry frying pan over medium heat for 2 minutes until fragrant. Add 2 tbsp oil, the cinnamon, sugar and salt. Cook, stirring, for 1 minute, then transfer to a plate to cool."},{"text":"In a small non-stick frying pan, lightly beat the eggs with salt and pepper. Melt 30 g butter and scramble the eggs gently until soft and creamy, about 3 minutes. Transfer to a plate."},{"text":"Preheat the oven to 180°C. In a bowl, gently toss the shredded chicken with the herbs."},{"text":"Lay a sheet of filo on a clean work surface and brush lightly with melted butter. Layer a second sheet on top and brush again. This is your base, it will be half the pastilla."},{"text":"Spread half the scrambled eggs in a rough rectangle on the lower half of the filo stack, leaving a 5 cm border. Top with half the chicken mixture, then half the almonds."},{"text":"Fold the sides of the filo inward, then roll up tightly from the bottom, creating a tight cylinder. Place seam-side down on a baking tray and brush the top with melted butter."},{"text":"Repeat steps 5 to 7 with the remaining filo, eggs, chicken and almonds to make a second pastilla."},{"text":"Bake for 25 to 30 minutes until the pastilla is deep golden brown and crisp."},{"text":"Let rest for 2 minutes, then transfer to a serving plate. Dust generously with icing sugar and cinnamon at the table. Serve hot."}],"id":"96d23ca0-4e21-4045-91e8-82ede59ade0d"},{"notes":["This salsa keeps for up to a week in an airtight container in the fridge.","Don't rush the toasting, low heat brings out the deep, complex flavours that make this salsa special."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat a heavy frying pan or comal over medium-low heat. Place the dried chillies in the pan and toast them, turning every 20 seconds, until fragrant and darkened but not blackened, about 4 to 5 minutes total. They should smell rich and earthy, almost like chocolate."},{"text":"Remove the stems and most of the seeds from the cooled chillies, shaking out what comes loose. Leave a few seeds for heat."},{"text":"In the same dry pan, toast the garlic cloves, turning occasionally, until lightly charred and soft, about 3 minutes."},{"text":"Using a molcajete (lava rock mortar and pestle), grind the toasted chillies, garlic, and salt together in slow, circular motions. Work the pestle in a grinding motion until you reach a coarse paste, this takes patience, but the friction releases the true flavour. Stop when you reach your preferred texture; some like it smooth, some leave it rough."},{"text":"Taste and add more salt if needed. If the salsa is too thick, stir in a few drops of water."}],"id":"33650227-138f-4226-bba5-2fbfdc42a3ae"},{"notes":["Make ahead: cook dried beans the day before, or use quality tinned beans. Refried beans reheat beautifully on the stove with a splash of water.","The texture depends on mashing technique and cook time, not the fat. If they seem too thick after cooling, warm and stir in a little hot water.","Serve with warm tortillas, as part of breakfast, or alongside grilled vegetables."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil in a heavy-bottomed pan over medium heat. Add the diced onion and cook, stirring occasionally, until soft and translucent, about 5 minutes."},{"text":"Add the minced garlic and cook for 1 minute, stirring, until fragrant."},{"text":"Add the drained beans to the pan, stirring to coat them in the oil and aromatics."},{"text":"Mash the beans with the back of a wooden spoon or a potato masher as they warm, breaking down the cell walls gradually. This is the key, you're not trying to purée them all at once, but rather let the starch and pressure do the work over time."},{"text":"Continue cooking and mashing over medium-low heat for about 8–10 minutes, adding splashes of water (or bean cooking liquid if you have it) as needed to reach a creamy, spreadable consistency. The beans should hold together but not be stiff."},{"text":"Taste and season with salt and cumin if using. The beans are ready when they're smooth, glossy, and slightly darker in colour from the slow breakdown of their starches."}],"id":"3d639bf4-e5db-450c-8fd7-190b1a4eb35a"},{"notes":["The filling can be made up to 4 hours ahead and kept in the fridge. Assemble the popiah just before serving so the skin stays soft.","Popiah are best eaten fresh, but you can wrap them tightly in plastic wrap and refrigerate for up to 2 hours. Don't freeze, the skin will crack when thawed.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C. Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through."],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch the turnip matchsticks in boiling salted water for 1 minute, then drain well and squeeze dry with a clean cloth. Set aside."},{"text":"Heat the oil in a wok or large pan over high heat. Add the garlic and cook for 30 seconds until fragrant."},{"text":"Add the pork mince and fry, breaking it up as it cooks, for 2 minutes until cooked through."},{"text":"Stir in the chopped prawn and cook for 1 minute until just cooked and still tender."},{"text":"Add the blanched turnip, oyster sauce, soy sauce and salt. Toss everything together for 1 minute, then remove from heat and let cool to room temperature."},{"text":"While the filling cools, make the sauce: stir together the hoisin, sweet chilli sauce, lime juice, sesame oil and minced garlic. Set aside."},{"text":"Lay one popiah skin flat on a clean surface. Spread a thin line of the filling across the middle, don't overfill. Top with a small handful of fresh coriander and a few pieces of spring onion."},{"text":"Fold the two sides of the skin over the filling, then roll tightly from the bottom up, tucking in the sides as you go. Place seam-side down on a plate."},{"text":"Repeat with the remaining skins and filling."},{"text":"Arrange the popiah on a serving plate, sprinkle with crispy fried shallots, and serve with the sauce on the side for dipping."}],"id":"8523f6b4-f6df-4fb7-8814-57f21ab434e5"},{"notes":["Make ahead: shape and refrigerate the patties up to 4 hours before frying.","Serve with a simple green salad or steamed beets, Hamburg style.","Food safety: Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through."],"steps":[{"text":"Bring a pot of salted water to the boil. Cut the potato into chunks and cook until just tender, about 12 minutes. Drain and let cool slightly."},{"text":"Meanwhile, check the fish for any fine bones and discard. Finely chop the onion."},{"text":"Grate the cooled potato into a large bowl. Finely chop or pulse the fish in a food processor until roughly textured but not a paste, then add to the potato."},{"text":"Add the onion, egg, breadcrumbs, mustard, salt and white pepper. Mix gently with your hands until just combined, don't overwork it."},{"text":"Shape the mixture into 8 patties about 7 cm across and 1.5 cm thick. Rest for 5 minutes if time allows."},{"text":"Heat a thin layer of oil in a large frying pan over medium heat. When hot, fry the patties in two batches, about 3–4 minutes each side, until golden and cooked through. Don't move them around; let them develop a good crust."},{"text":"Serve warm with a dollop of Dijon mustard on the side or a squeeze of lemon."}],"id":"fd74aee6-63f4-4b7f-87b2-4c22b503f587"},{"notes":["Make these up to 4 hours ahead and reheat wrapped in foil in a 180°C oven for 10 minutes.","The dough will keep in the fridge, covered, for up to 2 days. Bring to room temperature before rolling.","Cook pork until it reaches at least 70°C in the thickest part (75°C for mince and sausages)."],"id":"7c675e03-7c7c-4ba7-a33a-2a98499eaee9"},{"notes":["Slice the pork thinly while it's cold, pop it in the freezer for 30 minutes first if it's hard to cut.","Pickled chilies are the soul of this dish. Look for them in a tin in the Asian aisle, not the fresh chilli section.","This reheats beautifully; store leftovers in the fridge and warm gently in a wok or pan with a splash of water.","Food safety: Cook fresh pork to at least 70°C in the thickest part."],"id":"c7041581-50ca-4e52-86af-54fa4cf685a1"},{"notes":["The pickled vegetables keep for up to 5 days in the fridge, so you can make them ahead.","Press the tofu well, this is what makes it absorb flavour and fry up crisp. If you skip this step, you will have a sad bánh mì.","Leftover fried tofu keeps in an airtight container for 2 days and can be reheated gently in a low oven."],"id":"47799599-53bd-48e9-a8ff-070dc9349314"},{"steps":[{"text":"Bring a large pot of salted water to the boil. Add the bean sprouts and cook for 4 minutes, they should be tender but still have a slight bite. Drain well and spread on a clean kitchen towel to cool completely."},{"text":"Toast the sesame seeds in a small dry pan over medium heat, stirring often, for 2 minutes until fragrant. Transfer to a mortar and pestle and crush lightly, leaving some seeds whole."},{"text":"In a large bowl, whisk together the soy sauce, sesame oil and ginger. Add the cooled sprouts and toss gently to coat. Taste and adjust salt if needed."},{"text":"Sprinkle the toasted sesame over the top just before serving."}],"id":"53d74f14-3828-4522-b783-238c4ab49e64"},{"notes":["The crust is everything. Don't rush it, you want deep gold, not pale. If it's not crackling by minute 25, turn heat up slightly.","Use day-old cooked chicken if you have it; rotisserie works perfectly. The yoghurt binds everything and keeps the rice from drying out.","Make ahead: prepare the rice and yoghurt mixture up to 4 hours in advance. Cook in the pan just before serving."],"id":"f3c904a6-48d0-4bfe-9a4c-9eabc937bf4c"},{"notes":["The batter must ferment 8 hours or overnight, start the day before.","If you don't have an appam pan, use a shallow non-stick frying pan with a lid; the pancakes will be less lacy but still delicious.","The stew can be made up to 2 days ahead and warmed through before serving.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C."],"id":"fb87136c-432a-4e59-8f8f-4a1e16197b82"}]$q$::jsonb)), up as (update public.published_recipes p set recipe = p.recipe || (d.r - 'id' - 'notes') || case when d.r ? 'notes' then jsonb_build_object('notes', coalesce((select jsonb_agg(n) from jsonb_array_elements(d.r->'notes') n where btrim(n#>>'{}') <> 'Food safety:'),'[]'::jsonb)) else '{}'::jsonb end, diets = case when d.r ? 'diets' then array(select jsonb_array_elements_text(d.r->'diets')) else p.diets end from d where p.id=(d.r->>'id')::uuid returning p.id) select (select count(*) from d) expected, (select count(*) from up) updated;